ation so post 2018 with  the passage of the farm bill what were  sort of the initial applications that  these phases were used for  and then more recently as the the field  has expanded and the number of chiral  cannabinoids have been identified  what sort of applications these phases  have been used for for those types of  separations  so let's start first with the uh the  background  so as you may or may not know from your  history books or from  billy joel or some other source  uh thalidomide was sort of the  first case if you will  where the  structure of the molecule was really  pinpointed as being important for its  function in the body  so in the late 1950s and the early 60s  thalidomide was being prescribed to uh  pregnant mothers for uh assisting with  morning sickness  but as their children were being born  they were being born with some pretty  severe birth defects and so  uh sort of later on down the line it was  it was found that the r enantiomer which  is up on the screen as the left molecule  uh was the therapeutic and the s  enantiomer on the right  was the um  the one that was causing the the severe  birth defects but thalidomide wasn't  necessarily the first case where  this sort of chirality thing was noticed  or was uh  was noted as being important there were  a number of earlier accounts  sort of  sharing the differences that were  arising from natural structures so the  french physicist biatt  noticed that there was some optical  activity in the structure of quartz  there was actually two different  handedness  forms of the crystalline structure of  quartz  of course you might be familiar with  louis pasteur and his experiments with  tartaric acid  unfortunately he had the task of  separating them by hand  today it's much more convenient for the  separation of these enantiomers  historically because of solidimide and  because of a number of other cases where  the structure of a compound equaled some  sort of a  physical  indication in the body the  pharmaceutical industry has been one of  the greatest adapters and users of the  chiral chromatography the polysaccharide  based chirochromatography products it's  estimated something like eighty percent  of all drugs that are developed uh  whether they are commercialized or not  it's a different story but roughly  eighty percent of the drugs that are  considered for one indication or another  contain some sort of a chiral center so  remembering back to  chemistry high school chemistry days or  the dreaded organic chemistry days of  undergrad  a chiral center we're talking about a  tetrahedral carbon so carbon that has  four different things attached to it  but carbon is not the only chiral center  nitrogen sulfur and phosphorus can also  be  chiral depending upon what substituents  they have attached to them  a lot of different cases in the  literature that are pharmaceuticals  things like non-steroidal and  anti-inflammatory drugs beta blockers  antidepressants and many many more  i just have a few examples structures on  the screen here on the left hand side is  ace butylol which is a beta blocker  in the center is cetalopram which is an  ssri  and on the right hand side is fluoxetine  or prozac which is also an ssri  now i talked about the  some of the the services that are i'm a  part of here at chiro technologies one  of them being  the method development screening  there are a number of interactions that  can take place between the chiral  analyte and the chiral stationary phase  the chiral stationary phases that we're  talking about are either cellulose or  amylose-based  and those cellulose and amylose polymers  form a left-handed helical twist  structure and in doing so they form what  we like to call chiral grooves and think  about dna double-stranded dna  where the the groove is where a lot of  the interaction can take place with  outside  compounds coming in  so left-handed helical twists these  chiral grooves within them have what we  call chiral selectors and there's these  small  phenyl groups either phenyl carbamates  or benzo benzoates  that can form a number of intermolecular  interactions between the chiral  stationary phase and the analyte i  summarized here in the table  some of the main  interaction interactive forces that take  place between the chiral stationary  phases and the analytes  the ones that we're most interested in  usually are hydrogen bonding steer  hindrance pi pi interaction and dipole  dipole  generally speaking the others take place  but don't have as much of an influence  on  the actual separation of a compound  and the three-point interaction model  shown to the right  which is really a sort of a drug binding  model but it represents the point  what we look for is at least three  points of interaction on the chiral  stationary phase that line up with  functional groups that are on the active  or the inactive enantiomer or the  enantiomer that is going to be more  strongly retained or eluded earlier in  the hplc method and so if there is a  lining up of those functional groups  with the points of interaction on the  chiral stationary phase  the opposite enantiomer although it has  the same functional groups as you can  see  in the center of that right figure the  inactive enantiomer they don't line up  as well with the points of interaction  on the chiral stationary phase so  there's a difference in energy that  takes place between active and inactive  and we hope that there is enough of an  energy difference that eventually  propagates into a chiral separation  and even if you were to take that  inactive enantiomer and rotate it  there's still not a match up of those  points of interaction so still enough of  an energy difference to see a separation  of the of the enantiomers  in reality it's a lot more complicated  than that because you have to consider  everything as solvated by mobile phase  uh if you have a polar organic solvent  present like ethanol or methanol there's  certainly going to be blocking or  promoting of certain interactions  so what we like to do is we start off  with a method development screening  which allows us to check the chiral  analyte on all of the different  polysaccharide phases under a wide range  of different mobile phase combinations  and that usually is sufficient enough  then to help us find a chiral separation  to be able to get begin our method  development process  now for  cannabinoids  as i sort of laid the foundation in the  introduction cannabinoids initially post  2018 in the the farm bill  really the focus was on  quantifying or identifying the presence  of or lack thereof of thc  in cbd products so the farm bill  essentially legalized the regulated  production of hemp  and by extension the production of cbd  or cannabidiol which is a non-controlled  substance according to the dea  but it had the caveat of course that all  cbd products needed to contain less than  0.3 percent of delta 9  tetrahydrocannabinol  delta 9 thc so that was sort of the  initial target application and  many of the  a chiral phases that were available  c18 for instance were  widely known and cost effective based on  previous technologies that were  established and they worked well because  cbd and thc are not chiral molecules of  each other  they are not structurally related and so  things like c18 or phenyl or amine or  other commonly available commercialized  achirophases work very well and  probably know this very well there's a  number of application notes and papers  and posters and so forth on this topic  those phases work really well and the  methods have been  integrated in a lot of different labs  across the the us  now interestingly enough although  i set the stage for chiral stationary  phases and chiral separations  the chiral environment that's created by  the left-handed twist of these  polysaccharides  is also capable of doing eight chiral  separations as well which makes them  sort of dual purpose they can 