lized amylose method  if we shift gears a little bit and and  do just a screening of it we'll find  some very interesting results that help  us improve upon the retention that was  sort of set forth in in those other  methods  as you may or may not know  cannabis chromine can undergo a uv  cyclization process whether it be  naturally or as a result of storage  conditions to canon by cyclolog i have  the structure of both of those shown  here as sort of the uv  degradation process  up in the left hand corner the structure  of canada by chromine has the chiral  center starred it's alpha to the  the oxygen in the sex-membered ring  and of course when it goes and is  cyclized that chiral center is not lost  it is maintained  and you get the structure that is shown  in the bottom right of the slide for the  cannabis cyclone so if we mix these two  things together  or on the right hand side canada by  chromine by itself and we do that of  method development screening  on immobilized cellulose tris 35  dimethyl phenyl carbonate it will roll  off your tongue the more you say it  with 97 hexane 3 ethanol  you get a nice baseline resolution of  cbc and on the left-hand side mix it in  with the canon by psyclaw uh a nice four  peak separation again it's a chiral  mixed chiral a chiral separation as  you're achieving uh the separation of  both things and really the side-by-side  comparison here is to help  show illusion order so the cannabis  cyclol is very well resolved coming out  peak one and peak four and the cannabis  chromine is the third and second and  third peaks respectively  if we start looking at  thc itself thc naturally occurs only as  one enantiomer however it does have the  potential to form both the plus and  minus delta nine variant  more synthetically than naturally  occurring but nevertheless it also has  the same potential to develop the minus  and plus enantiomers for the delta eight  thc  uh delta eight thc now becoming quite  popular in the um in the industry as a  product as a that's available because  it's not a control it's not a controlled  substance so it's sort of a workaround  for the dea restrictions  but if we mix these four things together  again this would be a mixed chiral a  chiral separation between delta 8 delta  9 a chiral and then plus minus chiral  we get a nice baseline resolution of all  four enantiomers or four  isomers on amylose tris three  chloro4methylphenyl carbamate with 95  hexane and 5  ipa  again these are all now non-acidic  cannabinoids so there's no need for an  acidic additive like we saw when we were  dealing with thc a  a for instance  some of the other thc variants now also  uh occurring or showing up a little bit  more frequently delta six thc and delta  ten thc uh really it's it's the longer  names that i have shown on the  pictures here but it's a little easier  just to say delta 6 and delta delta 10  for presentation purposes  but much like delta 8 and delta 9 it's a  mixed chiral a chiral separation between  the chiral delta 6 and delta 10  separately and the a chiral mixture  together  we have a nice baseline resolution for  that under normal phase hplc conditions  the immobilized cellulose tris  3-5-dimethylphenyl carbamate is as you  might have gathered so far a very useful  chiral separa chiral stationary phase  for these sorts of separations  it shows a very wide range of  selectivity across a number of different  cannabinoid mixtures i can't necessarily  call it a cannabinoid column but it does  a very good job at separating a lot of  these different mixtures  but in any case here for delta 6 and  delta 10 you see here  sort of an interesting illusion order  where you've got the delta 6 variants  coming out at 3 4 peaks 3 and 4 and then  the delta 10 variance coming out at  peaks one and peaks two  but if we go and we switch up the chiral  stationary phase to  uh cellulose tris three chloro5 methyl  phenyl carbonate  uh which is chiropac ig you get a little  bit of the shuffling of the aleutian  order so you get delta uh six  and delta  the the two enantiomers of delta six  coming out one and two now and the two  enantiomers of delta ten coming out  three and four  uh i mentioned that only because uh for  preparative separations it's it's always  a good handle to be able to change  illusion order generally speaking your  peaks of interest you want to elute  first so should you want to adapt this  to a preparative chiral separation  having the ability to mix or switch  around the aleutian order is kind of  important for that  so where are we headed just a couple of  closing remarks here where we headed  not surprisingly i would say that  as time goes on and the legal status of  a lot of these different cannabinoids  begins to evolve that you're going to  start to see a lot of additional  regulation  much like was the case early on with  needing to quantify the percentage of  thc in cbd products it would not  surprise me if the regulation evolves to  needing to measure the level of delta 8  thc for instance  hhc which is hexahydrocannabinol  cannabidiol has now become another  workaround for the legal status of delta  9 thc  we have a number of  applications that have been developed on  on the separations of hhc now as well  but having proper testing for that  the presence of opposite enantiomers  might also become  something of interest  cbd for instance is  not only commercially available but it's  also prescribed as a pharmaceutical  and so the need for  characterizing the opposite enantiomer  much like was the case for thalidomide  might also arise for cbd or any of the  other cannabinoids that become  registered pharmaceuticals for different  applications  and so the take-home message for this  presentation really is that chiral  columns can can do it all  as these sort of regulations do become  more relevant or more prominent  a chiral methods that don't separate out  the enantiomers aren't going to be  sufficient anymore and so  we have hopefully started to lay the  groundwork for those  those applications  a lot of what you have seen here is all  nice clean analytical standards so  for us and sort of for application  development the next step is taking  these and putting them into real world  samples and kind of messing the samples  up a little bit and complicating them  and adding in other cannabinoids and  things like that to see  how exactly  we can go about using these for for  accurate quantification and  identification  uh if you like the information that  you've heard today um if you want to  reach out to me and and have a broader  discussion on chirochromatography or a  more specific one on these applications  please feel free to reach out to me my  email address is shown on the screen now  wombstead chiral cti.daisel.com  or feel free to give me a call on my  office phone  extension 264  i would love to have a conversation with  you about these and other applications  of  chirochromatography so i appreciate it  very much again for  your time and attention today and i look  forward to discussing these with you  more  in the near future thank you  you

